// from string (truncating at N, length byte updated)" rule, implemented
// with runtime/clarus/str.cla's rtStrStore(dst: ptr, dstcap: int, src: ptr)
// (a clamped Pascal-string-buffer copy -- exactly the shape a raw xrec
// field address and an ordinary Str255-representation Clarus string both
// already are, Ch3: "Strings are length-prefixed Pascal strings"). Args
// (both intrinsics): (fieldAddr: EXRecAddr, N: int const, ...) -- READ
// takes just those two, is the intrinsic's own RESULT (a fresh strT(N)
// value -- Tasks 5-6 realize this the same way cprint.cla's other
// string-producing expressions already materialize a temp, e.g. string
// concatenation/slicing's own scratch-temp printing); WRITE additionally
// takes a third arg (the source string expression) and is an ExprStmt
// (void), mirroring ITextStore's statement-position shape. Deliberately
// NOT SStoreStr/newIRStoreStr: that statement's `dst` is printed as a
// plain C lvalue (fpStoreStr takes `&dst`), but an EXRecAddr's own C
// rendering IS ALREADY an address -- reusing SStoreStr would need a
// dst-kind special case in every backend's printer for one field kind;
// two dedicated intrinsics keep the existing StoreStr contract untouched.
